{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":2,"output":8,"cacheRead":14973,"cacheWrite":2474,"totalTokens":17457,"cost":{"input":2.0000000000000002e-7,"output":0.000004,"cacheRead":0.00014973,"cacheWrite":0.00030925,"total":0.00046318,"totalFromProvider":false},"cacheWrite1h":0},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I'm keeping the canonical str output as \"underline2\" since the existing tests expect it, and leaving str and repr unchanged. Next I'll edit style.py: add a double_underline argument to the docstring, and register \"double_underline\", \"double-underline\", and \"du\" in STYLE_ATTRIBUTES so they map to the double_underline keyword.\n\nI'm weighing how to handle a conflict between the two kwargs: `double_underline` and `underline2` both control the same bit, so I'm considering which one wins when both are given, leaning toward whichever is explicitly set. The parse path maps `not double_underline` to a False value through the same attribute table, which should work without extra changes. I still need to check that markup tags with hyphens like \"double-underline red\" are accepted.\n\nFor the HTML output, I'll emit only the double underline when double_underline is set, falling back to the plain underline otherwise, since the double form is the more specific of the two. I'm keeping the existing pattern of appending one text-decoration rule per attribute rather than merging them, so strike behaves as before. The SVG export will follow the same logic with its own style string.\n\nI'm checking that the `_Bit` descriptor reads from `self._attributes` as I expect, so the new flag works like the existing ones.\n\n"}}
